+# Canonical capture-session and slice ACK ledger
+
+**Feature:** `capture-session-ack-ledger`
+**Issue:** `ce3127d7-2ae0-4eff-aa37-1101762d0d0b`
+**Date:** 2026-08-10
+**Classification:** Critical / T3 — database schema, tenant authority, raw-audio durability, retention, and deletion
+**Status:** PLAN draft — H1-H5 are open; not DEV-ready
+**Human approval state:** No H1-H4 decision is recorded and H5 DEV approval is absent. This document is design evidence only.
+**Base:** `origin/main@5a4ed8d8f52b7fe78044fac000602b9cd6552ee0`
+**Branch/worktree:** `codex/capture-session-ack-ledger-plan` / `.worktrees/capture-session-ack-ledger-plan`
+
+## Purpose
+
+Create the one canonical Neon authority that makes meeting capture retryable without lying to clients. A server ACK must mean that the exact raw slice is durably readable and the tenant-bound slice ledger transaction has committed. Speech-to-text (STT) runs later from durable work and is safe to reclaim after crashes. No capture authority may live in `jobs.payload`, process memory, a browser-only counter, or another database.
+
+This issue is the mandatory server/database prerequisite for `d88d461a-c259-4dc6-a751-f2b3c6898b7a` (local meeting-capture durability). The live Forge dependency is corrected: `d88` is blocked by `ce3127`; `ce3127` has `d88` as a dependent.
+
+## Verified baseline
+
+At the exact base SHA:
+
+- Neon `neondb.public` is the sole supported database. `packages/db/src/schema.ts` plus `packages/db/src/meeting-schema.ts` are one Drizzle model, and `packages/db/migrations` is the only live migration root (`docs/architecture/schema-domain-ownership.md`).
+- The Drizzle journal ends at `0019_neon_authority_reconciliation`; migration parity passes. Meeting Authority PR A is Forge issue `2660c6a4-3e8d-4f4e-b3bb-eb61446c3c98`, currently blocked and not landed. Its latest plan evidence is docs-only; the exact landed authority SHA is **UNSET / must be verified from live `origin/main` at H1**. This plan therefore does **not** reserve a migration number.
+- `meetings` and `transcript_segments` still have nullable tenant/owner columns at this base (`packages/db/src/meeting-schema.ts:61-100`). Capture cannot invent a replacement identity mapping.
+- `audio_assets` stores a random object path and retention timestamp but has no capture session, sequence, digest, or ACK (`packages/db/src/meeting-schema.ts:262-272`).
+- `/meetings/{meeting_id}/transcribe` archives an optional raw chunk, synchronously calls STT, inserts transcript segments, then returns the client-supplied chunk index (`apps/meeting-api/backend/server.py:2886-3001`). It is not an ACK protocol.
+- Current R2 object keys contain a random UUID (`apps/meeting-api/backend/services/storage.py:42-45`), so a retry creates another object. Current job writes are completion records, not claimable work (`apps/meeting-api/backend/server.py:1686-1732`).
+- Existing tests are healthy after the declared Bun dependencies are installed: contracts 62 passed; SDK 8 passed; Meeting API 236 passed/1 skipped; DB lint, typecheck, and 34 tests passed; migration parity passed.
+
+## Success criteria
+
+1. One versioned contract defines capture-session identity, slice sequence/digest, raw acceptance states, STT work states, ACK/status/finalize behavior, retention/deletion behavior, and redacted error codes.
+2. Neon/Drizzle owns three normalized relations: capture sessions, capture slices, and durable slice-transcription work. `jobs.payload` is never authoritative and no second journal/database is introduced.
+3. Every session and child row is tenant-bound. The authenticated server derives tenant and actor; request bodies cannot choose either. Current meeting membership/access is rechecked through the exact landed authority primitive selected at Human Gate H1.
+4. `(tenant_id, capture_session_id, sequence)` is unique; sequence is non-negative and starts at zero. A retry with the same SHA-256 digest is idempotent. A retry at the same identity with a different digest fails closed without changing the accepted row or raw object.
+5. Raw storage uses a deterministic content-addressed key and conditional create. The persisted read handle contains provider/bucket/key/ETag (and checksum metadata), never a presigned URL. An ACK is returned only after the conditional raw write is durably readable and the final Neon transaction commits the accepted slice plus pending STT work.
+6. Slice acceptance has explicit `reserved`, `processing`, `acknowledged`, and `failed` recovery states. These states describe raw acceptance only; STT state is separate and cannot manufacture or revoke an ACK.
+7. The final ACK transaction row-locks the session, validates identity/digest/raw handle, acknowledges the slice, inserts the unique pending STT work row, advances the contiguous ACK watermark, and returns the committed digest/watermark. A process crash after commit but before response is recoverable by exact replay.
+8. Status returns the committed contiguous watermark and compact missing/non-acknowledged ranges. Gaps are derived from rows and the latched terminal sequence, not stored as a mutable JSON list.
+9. The first valid finalize request immutably latches `terminal_sequence` and intent. A different terminal sequence is always rejected. Finalization becomes terminal only when every sequence `0..terminal_sequence` is acknowledged; no later slice can be reserved above the latch.
+10. Every acknowledged slice has one durable STT work row. Workers claim bounded batches with leases; pending and expired-processing rows are reclaimable at startup. Duplicate delivery cannot duplicate transcript segments or completion.
+11. Consent, capture provenance, retention, and deletion data are immutable or monotonic. A retention/deletion worker cannot delete an object while STT holds a live lease. The unresolved product/legal policies at H2-H4 stop DEV rather than being guessed.
+12. Unit, catalog, concurrency, crash-point, security, and real-Neon tests prove the schema and transactions. Required real-Neon validation fails closed when credentials, nonzero execution, exact-head evidence, or cleanup is missing.
+13. After landing and verification on current `origin/main`, the issue publishes the exact landed SHA, migration tag/floor, contract version, and canonical adapter paths. `d88` may enter DEV only from that handoff.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Browser IndexedDB/outbox, recorder sequencing, retry UI, and local Blob deletion. Those remain in `d88`.
+- Synchronous STT in the ingest request, summarization, candidates, work items, meeting-memory materialization, or `meeting.capture.completed/v1` consumers.
+- Provider SDKs, RealtimeKit/Recall/LiveKit, diarization, audio enhancement, or desktop capture.
+- Production Neon access, migration application, credential/role rotation, deployment, push, PR, or merge during this PLAN.
+- Supabase/Alembic/raw Meeting migrations, another schema/journal, or a `jobs.payload` fallback.
+- Inventing consent wording, source-kind vocabulary, retention duration, deletion-on-terminal-STT-failure policy, or identity mappings.
+
+## Selected approach
+
+### Same-Neon transactional ledger plus content-addressed raw storage
+
+Use Neon as the transaction authority and R2/local storage only as the raw byte store behind a narrow immutable-handle adapter:
+
+```text
+authenticated request
+  -> authorize meeting and derive tenant/actor
+  -> reserve/claim the sequence row in Neon (not ACK), under session-row lock then slice-row lock
+  -> stream bytes through a bounded replayable spool while the server computes SHA-256 + byte length
+  -> compare (but never trust) the client digest
+  -> winning reservation_fence CAS latches server digest + length once, changing state out of reserved
+  -> derive tenant/session/sequence/digest storage key
+  -> conditional PUT deterministic object key; verify provider checksum/size/ETag
+  -> BEGIN Neon transaction
+       lock capture_session
+       lock slice after the session; recheck terminal/status and all reservation fences
+       mark slice acknowledged
+       insert unique pending STT work row
+       advance contiguous ACK watermark
+       call the same locked maybe_finalize invariant used by finalize
+     COMMIT
+  -> return ACK(session, sequence, digest, watermark)
+
+independent worker
+  -> claim pending/expired work with lease
+  -> open immutable raw stream from stored handle
+  -> transcribe
+  -> one transaction locks session -> slice -> work, checks the STT fence,
+     inserts idempotent slice-derived segments, and completes work
+```
+
+The raw write and Neon commit cannot be one distributed transaction. The design closes that gap with deterministic identity, conditional write, durable intermediate states, and replay:
+
+- stream failure before the latch releases the reservation back to `reserved`; raw write failure after the latch leaves a retryable `processing|failed` row with immutable digest/length and no ACK;
+- crash after raw write before Neon ACK transaction: retry HEADs the same key, validates digest/size, and completes the transaction;
+- crash after Neon commit before HTTP response: same-digest retry reads the committed ACK;
+- crash after ACK before any in-memory scheduling: pending work already exists in the ACK transaction and a startup sweep claims it.
+
+The selected database access model is DB-enforced: the Meeting runtime role has **no direct `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE` grant on the capture ledger**. Narrow, `SECURITY DEFINER` transaction functions with a fixed `search_path` are the only runtime write boundary. The functions re-check the H1 meeting authority and tenant/actor context, and PostgreSQL triggers/checks enforce immutable fields, monotonic state/timestamps/watermarks, non-negative bounds, and fence advancement even when a caller bypasses repository code. Migration/admin roles own the functions and constraints; runtime roles cannot create functions, change ownership, `SET ROLE`, or grant themselves ledger access. Real-Neon probes must attempt direct table DML and forged function context as the runtime role and prove denial.
+
+All session operations use one lock order: `capture_sessions FOR UPDATE` first, then the matching `capture_slices FOR UPDATE`; reserve, ACK, and finalize re-check session status and a latched `terminal_sequence` after taking the session lock. The shared locked `maybe_finalize` invariant is called by both the ACK transaction and finalize, so no caller can finalize against a stale watermark or terminal decision.
+
+Every reclaimable side effect carries a monotonic fence: `reservation_fence` on a slice, `work_fence` on STT work, and `deletion_fence` on deletion claims. A claim increments its fence; raw acceptance, STT completion, and deletion/tombstone updates are compare-and-set operations that include the winning fence. A stale owner may report a retryable loss but cannot mutate bytes, ACK state, transcript segments, or tombstones.
+
+Cloudflare documents that successful R2 writes are synchronously durable and strongly read-after-write consistent. R2 is last-writer-wins for an ordinary repeated key, so the adapter must use `If-None-Match: *` conditional create and validate an existing object's stored checksum/size on replay. R2 bucket versioning/object lock is not assumed.
+
+The server, not the browser, is authoritative for bytes: it streams the request through a bounded replayable spool, computes SHA-256 and byte length, compares an optional client digest for mismatch detection, and latches those values with the winning reservation fence exactly once before deriving `capture/{tenant_id}/{capture_session_id}/{sequence}/{server_digest}`. The provider checksum and HEAD size must match the latched values before the handle is persisted. Retries stream/recompute first and compare to the latched digest/length; they never replace them. The adapter rejects handles whose provider/bucket/key scope does not match the tenant/session/sequence/digest, so no object can be shared across tenants.
+
+### Why not the alternatives
+
+1. **`jobs.payload` as the ledger:** rejected. It mixes unrelated job lifecycle with raw acceptance, has no session/sequence/digest constraints, and violates the issue boundary.
+2. **Synchronous transcribe-before-ACK:** rejected. Provider latency/failure controls durability, a post-storage crash loses scheduling intent, and duplicate delivery can duplicate transcript rows.
+3. **Object-store event as authority:** rejected. It creates a second control plane and cannot enforce tenant/session/finalize invariants transactionally with Neon.
+
+## Canonical data contract
+
+Names below are locked logical names. The migration tag is resolved only after rebasing at DEV.
+
+### `capture_sessions`
+
+| Field | Contract |
+| --- | --- |
+| `id uuid` | Opaque server-generated `capture_session_id`; primary key. |
+| `tenant_id text` | Required, server-derived tenant. Part of a unique `(tenant_id,id)` key for child FKs. |
+| `meeting_id text` | Required for the browser-capture lane. Same-tenant relationship must use the H1 authority primitive; no unverified cross-tenant FK or ID equality. |
+| `created_by_actor_id text` | Required server-derived internal actor/principal selected by H1. Never accepted from payload. |
+| `status` | `open`, `finalize_requested`, `finalized`, `deletion_requested`, `deleted`, `failed`; monotonic transitions only. |
+| `next_contiguous_ack_sequence bigint` | Nullable watermark (`NULL` means no sequence acknowledged; otherwise highest contiguous sequence from zero). Updated only while the session row is locked. |
+| `max_observed_sequence bigint` | Nullable high-water observation; monotonic, not proof of ACK. |
+| `finalize_mode` | `non_empty` or approved H4 `empty`; immutable once finalize intent is latched. |
+| `terminal_sequence bigint` | Nullable until first valid finalize; non-negative and immutable for `non_empty`; remains `NULL` for the explicit `empty` mode. |
+| `finalize_requested_at`, `finalized_at` | Server timestamps. `finalized_at` requires watermark = terminal sequence. |
+| `consent_actor_id`, `consent_recorded_at`, `consent_policy_version`, `consent_locale`, `consent_source_kinds` | Required immutable evidence. Exact source vocabulary and policy freshness come from H2. |
+| `source_kind`, `source_topology` | Immutable provenance validated by the shared contract; exact vocabulary comes from H2. |
+| `raw_retention_expires_at` | Immutable policy result for this session; nullable only if H3 explicitly approves indefinite retention. |
+| `deletion_requested_at`, `deleted_at` | Monotonic lifecycle evidence. No hard-delete of the ledger during the recovery/audit window. |
+| `created_at`, `updated_at` | Server timestamps; updates are limited to documented monotonic fields. |
+
+Constraints/indexes:
+
+- primary key `(id)` and unique `(tenant_id,id)`;
+- same-tenant meeting/actor relationships selected by H1;
+- checks for non-negative watermarks/terminal/max sequence, `finalize_mode`/terminal coherence, watermark not above terminal when latched, timestamp/state coherence, non-empty consent sources, and immutable-field triggers or repository guards backed by negative tests;
+- indexes for `(tenant_id,meeting_id,created_at)` and finalize/deletion sweep predicates.
+
+### `capture_slices`
+
+| Field | Contract |
+| --- | --- |
+| `id uuid` | Server-generated internal row identity. |
+| `tenant_id`, `capture_session_id` | Required composite FK to `capture_sessions(tenant_id,id)`. |
+| `sequence bigint` | Required, non-negative, zero-based monotonic client sequence. |
+| `digest_algorithm`, `content_digest` | Nullable only while `acceptance_state=reserved`; v1 is server-computed SHA-256 in canonical binary/hex form. The winning `reservation_fence` CAS latches it once before conditional PUT and it is immutable thereafter; a client digest is comparison metadata only. |
+| `byte_length bigint`, `mime_type`, `duration_ms bigint` | `byte_length` is nullable only while `acceptance_state=reserved`; the winning fence latches the server-computed non-negative length once and it is immutable thereafter. MIME/duration are validated metadata. |
+| `acceptance_state` | `reserved`, `processing`, `acknowledged`, `failed`. Only `acknowledged` is ACK-eligible. |
+| `reservation_owner`, `reservation_expires_at`, `reservation_fence`, `attempt_count` | Bounded recovery claim; each reclaim increments a unique monotonic fence and expired non-ACK work can be reclaimed. |
+| `storage_provider`, `storage_bucket`, `storage_key`, `storage_etag`, `storage_checksum` | Immutable raw read handle. No presigned/download URL is stored or returned as authority. |
+| `failure_code`, `retry_after` | Redacted machine code and retry schedule; never provider exception text or credentials. |
+| `acknowledged_at`, `created_at`, `updated_at` | Server timestamps. `acknowledged_at` is set only by the final ACK transaction. |
+| `deletion_state`, `deletion_claim_expires_at`, `deletion_fence`, `deleted_at` | Monotonic raw deletion lifecycle; each deletion claim increments a unique fence and exact terminal-failure eligibility comes from H3. |
+
+Constraints/indexes:
+
+- unique `(tenant_id,capture_session_id,sequence)`;
+- unique deterministic `storage_key` for non-null handles; the key is scoped as `tenant/session/sequence/server_digest`, so no cross-tenant object can be replayed;
+- same identity + same digest is replay; same identity + different digest is `CAPTURE_DIGEST_MISMATCH` and changes nothing;
+- `digest_algorithm`, `content_digest`, and `byte_length` may be NULL only in `reserved`; `processing`, `acknowledged`, and `failed` rows require all three latched values. A stream failure before latching returns to `reserved` rather than creating a digest-less `failed` row;
+- the winning `reservation_fence` CAS is the only transition that fills digest/length; retries compute bytes first and compare to the latched values, never replace them;
+- acknowledged rows require both latched digest/length plus a complete raw handle, provider checksum, and `acknowledged_at`; non-acknowledged rows cannot populate `acknowledged_at`;
+- no sequence above the session's latched terminal is accepted (transactional repository invariant);
+- claim indexes cover acceptance recovery and deletion sweeps; deletion claims exclude `deletion_requested|deletion_claimed` rows and are fenced by compare-and-set;
+- PostgreSQL triggers reject immutable-field changes, status regressions, timestamp/watermark decreases, fence reuse/decrease, and any direct ledger mutation not performed by the approved transaction function.
+
+### `capture_slice_transcription_work`
+
+| Field | Contract |
+| --- | --- |
+| `id uuid` | Work-row identity. |
+| `tenant_id`, `capture_slice_id` | Composite tenant-safe FK; unique per slice. |
+| `status` | `pending`, `processing`, `completed`, `retryable_failed`, `terminal_failed`. Independent of ACK. |
+| `available_at`, `lease_owner`, `lease_expires_at`, `claimed_at`, `work_fence` | Durable bounded claim fields; each STT reclaim increments a unique monotonic work fence. |
+| `attempt_count`, `last_error_code` | Monotonic attempt count and redacted code. |
+| `provider`, `model` | STT provenance selected server-side at processing time; not authority for ACK. |
+| `completed_at`, `created_at`, `updated_at` | Lifecycle timestamps with state-coherence checks. |
+
+Workers select only eligible `pending`/`retryable_failed` or expired `processing` rows in deterministic order with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, then set a lease and increment `work_fence` in the same transaction. PostgreSQL documents `SKIP LOCKED` as suitable for queue-like consumers, not general reads; all status/finalize reads use consistent ordinary queries and explicit session row locks.
+
+Transcript idempotency is relational, not an in-memory promise: `transcript_segments` receives `capture_slice_id` plus a stable provider/source segment ordinal or key, with a tenant-safe uniqueness constraint. Segment insertion and work completion commit together. Duplicate worker delivery returns the existing completed result.
+
+## State semantics
+
+### Slice ingest and replay
+
+1. Authorize the current actor/tenant/meeting and load the session by `(tenant_id,id)`.
+2. In one transaction, lock the session first, recheck status/terminal/consent and all server bounds, then lock or insert `(tenant,session,sequence)`; no path takes the slice lock first.
+3. For an existing acknowledged or latched row, stream/recompute the request bytes first; compare the server digest and length to the immutable latched values. A match returns the committed ACK without touching R2/STT; a mismatch is zero-mutation `CAPTURE_DIGEST_MISMATCH`.
+4. A concurrent first delivery that holds the same identity returns `CAPTURE_SLICE_IN_PROGRESS` with a bounded `Retry-After` (not a second raw write). A new/expired reservation increments `reservation_fence`; bytes are streamed before any digest/length is accepted.
+5. The winning reservation-fence CAS latches server-computed digest/length exactly once and transitions out of `reserved`. A client mismatch releases the reservation to `reserved`; it cannot populate or replace authoritative values.
+6. The route conditionally writes/read-verifies the key derived from the latched digest. A write failure leaves immutable digest/length in `processing|failed`; every retry streams bytes first and compares to those values before retrying the same key.
+7. The final transaction locks session then slice, revalidates latched digest/length, complete raw handle, reservation fence, and terminal latch, sets `acknowledged`, inserts the unique pending STT row, advances the watermark, calls locked `maybe_finalize`, and commits.
+8. Build the ACK only from committed values after commit. A DB exception, unknown commit outcome, or response serialization failure never manufactures a new ACK.
+
+### Watermark and gaps
+
+- Sequence zero is the first valid slice.
+- The watermark is `NULL` before sequence zero is acknowledged.
+- Each ACK transaction locks the session first, then slices in sequence order, and advances through consecutive acknowledged rows beginning at `watermark + 1`; concurrent out-of-order ACKs serialize on the session row.
+- Before finalize, status may report known non-acknowledged ranges through `max_observed_sequence`, clearly labeled as observed rather than terminal gaps.
+- After terminal latch, gaps are the compact ranges in `0..terminal_sequence` whose rows are missing or not acknowledged.
+- Gaps are computed under a consistent transaction and capped/paginated to prevent an attacker-supplied huge terminal sequence from causing unbounded work.
+
+### Finalize
+
+- Finalize requires an authenticated same-tenant session, a non-negative terminal sequence, and no known slice above that terminal. It locks the session first, then relevant slices, and rechecks the latch and status under that lock.
+- The first valid request atomically sets `terminal_sequence`, `finalize_requested_at`, and `status=finalize_requested`. It calls the same locked `maybe_finalize` invariant used by ACK; it may return `awaiting_gaps` with ranges.
+- Exact replay is idempotent. Any different terminal is `CAPTURE_TERMINAL_SEQUENCE_IMMUTABLE`.
+- New reservations above the latch fail. Existing reservations above the proposed latch make the first finalize request fail without latching.
+- When watermark equals terminal and every row is acknowledged, the shared locked `maybe_finalize` sets `finalized_at/status=finalized`. Empty-session finalization remains a human H4 choice and has an explicit encoding only after approval.
+- STT completion is not required for capture ACK finalization; it is required only for downstream transcript-complete semantics owned outside this issue.
+
+### Durable STT and startup recovery
+
+- The ACK transaction creates the pending work row; it never publishes only to an in-memory queue.
+- Startup and periodic sweeps claim pending, retryable-failed, and expired-processing rows in bounded batches, incrementing `work_fence` for each lease.
+- The worker obtains bytes only through `open_slice_stream(handle, expected_digest, expected_length)`. Missing, expired, checksum-mismatched, or unreadable objects move work to retryable/terminal failure according to the approved retry policy and never revoke ACK.
+- A duplicate worker or expired lease cannot commit a second transcript result. Completion CAS locks session -> slice -> work and checks the winning `work_fence` plus unique slice-segment keys.
+- RED crash cases include: after ACK before scheduling, process restart sweep, missing/expired object, duplicate worker delivery, and retention racing a live processing lease.
+
+## Consent, provenance, retention, and deletion
+
+- Consent is created with the session and immutable afterward. Ingest compares the session's approved policy/source topology; it does not trust repeated client claims.
+- Tenant, actor, meeting, and source provenance are server-derived or validated against the versioned contract. Logs contain opaque session/slice IDs, sequence, state, attempt count, and redacted codes only—never raw audio, transcript text, object URL, bucket credentials, token, email, or full provider exception.
+- Retention is computed once from the approved server policy and stored as an absolute timestamp. A later config change does not silently rewrite old evidence.
+- Deletion is an atomic fenced workflow. It locks the session (when session state is involved), then the slice, then the matching work row; claims increment `deletion_fence` and exclude `deletion_requested|deletion_claimed` rows. Raw deletion is allowed only when STT is `completed`, or `terminal_failed` under the explicit H3 approval; it is never allowed for `pending`, `retryable_failed`, or `processing`. The worker deletes the exact handle first, then performs a fence-matched tombstone CAS (`deleted_at`/`deletion_state`); a stale owner cannot mark deletion complete. A crash after object delete before ledger update is recovered by idempotent HEAD/not-found handling followed by the same fence-matched CAS.
+- The ledger/tombstone remains for the approved audit/recovery window after raw deletion. Whether terminal STT failure permits raw deletion and whether user deletion overrides retention are Human Gate H3 decisions.
+
+## Tenant and authorization boundary
+
+- Public request payloads never carry authoritative `tenant_id`, actor IDs, storage handles, ACK state, retention timestamps, leases, or worker status.
+- Session creation resolves authenticated provider subject to an internal user/actor and active tenant membership, then authorizes the meeting through the exact landed Meeting Authority primitive chosen at H1.
+- Every repository operation includes `(tenant_id,session_id)`; child FKs include tenant. Unknown, cross-tenant, stale membership, deleted meeting, and mismatched owner cases return one uniform denial/not-found envelope without revealing existence.
+- The Meeting runtime role receives no direct ledger DML; it can call only the narrow approved transaction functions and read the redacted status projection. Migration/admin roles remain separate, function `search_path` is fixed, and function ownership cannot be reassigned by runtime. PostgreSQL checks/triggers enforce immutable/monotonic transitions and fence CAS independently of Python. Negative real-Neon probes cover cross-tenant reads/writes, direct table DML, forged function context, DDL, role escalation, direct ACK forgery, reservation/work/deletion fence forgery, and lease forgery.
+
+## Idempotency matrix
+
+| Situation | Required result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Same session/sequence/digest after committed ACK | Return identical committed ACK and watermark; no new object/work. |
+| Same session/sequence, different digest | `CAPTURE_DIGEST_MISMATCH`; zero mutation. |
+| Concurrent identical first deliveries | One caller owns the reservation fence; a competing caller receives stable retryable `CAPTURE_SLICE_IN_PROGRESS` plus bounded `Retry-After`, then exact retry converges on one conditional object, one slice row, one work row, and one ACK. |
+| Raw object committed, DB transaction absent | Retry validates object and completes ACK transaction. |
+| DB commit outcome unknown | Re-read exact identity; return ACK only if committed row matches digest/handle. |
+| ACK committed, process crashes before scheduling | Startup sweep claims pre-existing pending work. |
+| Two STT workers claim/deliver | One winning `work_fence`/lease result; stale completion is a no-op and unique segment keys prevent duplicates. |
+| Finalize exact replay | Same terminal/status/gaps. |
+| Finalize with different terminal | Immutable-terminal error; no mutation. |
+| Delete races active STT lease | Fenced deletion claim locks slice/work and fails/defers for `pending`, `retryable_failed`, or `processing`; the object remains readable. |
+
+## Technical and security research
+
+- Neon supports real Postgres transactions; its serverless HTTP driver supports non-interactive multi-query transactions, while WebSocket/`pg` compatibility supports interactive transactions. Existing Meeting API uses a pooled PostgreSQL connection, so the repository should use one explicit connection/transaction for row locks and ACK commit. [Neon serverless driver](https://neon.com/docs/serverless/serverless-driver)
+- Neon pooling uses PgBouncer transaction mode. The design uses transaction-scoped row locks, not session-level advisory locks or session state. [Neon connection pooling](https://neon.com/docs/connect/connection-pooling)
+- Neon branches are appropriate for isolated migration and concurrency tests; the repository already has a required exact-head disposable-project/production-derived-branch DB contract lane. [Neon branching](https://neon.com/docs/guides/branching-intro)
+- PostgreSQL row locks last through transaction end and serialize competing updates to the session/slice rows. Serializable transactions require retry; the implementation may use explicit row locks under Read Committed for the narrow invariants, with deterministic retry for deadlock/serialization errors. [PostgreSQL transaction isolation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html), [explicit locking](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html)
+- `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` is reserved for the work/deletion queues. It is intentionally not used for status or authorization reads because it returns an inconsistent view. [PostgreSQL SELECT](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-select.html)
+- R2 successful writes are synchronously durable and strongly consistent, but ordinary same-key concurrent writes are last-writer-wins. R2 supports `If-None-Match` conditional `PutObject`; the adapter uses it with content-addressed keys. [R2 durability](https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/reference/durability/), [R2 consistency](https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/reference/consistency/), [R2 S3 compatibility](https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/api/s3/api/)
